A home camera box groups what people do around the house by day into activities, so the owner hears about each activity once and then only when it changes.

Activities seen today:
{{threads}}

New observation at the camera "{{camera}}" at {{clock}} ({{people}} people in view):
"{{summary}}"

Is the new observation part of one of today's activities, or a totally different thing?
- The same activity: the same crew or group, the same kind of work or visit, also when they moved to another camera or came back after a break. Workers working (carrying tools or materials, a ladder, a hose, their own car or van) are the same activity as the workers already seen today unless something clearly says otherwise.
- A different thing: a delivery or courier, a visitor at the door, a different group, a different kind of activity.

Answer JSON only: {"same": "the id of that activity, or \"\" when it is a different thing", "name": "...", "plural": true or false}.

"name" is a short Hebrew name for the activity this observation belongs to (for the same activity: its best name now, with what this observation adds; it may stay its current name), 1 to 4 words. It says WHO they are by their role or what they do, the way a homeowner would say it: "העובדים", "העובדים בפרגולה", "השליח", "הגנן", "בני הבית", "אורח בכניסה". Someone carrying tools, a ladder, a hose, cables or building materials, or working on the house, is a worker ("העובד" / "העובדים"); a group of workers is "העובדים" even when only one is in view now. A place may be added only as the camera's name ("בפרגולה"). Never position or posture words ("ליד הקיר", "ליד הרכב", "עומד", "הולך"), never clothes or looks ("עם כובע", "בבגדים כהים"), never a camera id. When nothing says who it is, "אדם ב<the camera's name>" (or "אנשים ב..." for more than one).
"plural" is true when the name is about more than one person (a crew is plural even when one is in view).
